7592186045417] [17592186362043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362043 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362043 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362043 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362043 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362043 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "431fc151-9f48-50ea-8f92-91e1f1278462" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186360845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186360849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362207 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd49cbaa-d57f-5e0e-9cec-db71bf257efe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362326 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362326 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98761" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362326 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362326 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements is true regarding the equilibrium solutions of ${y}^{′}=(y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362326 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f9765a7a-f1b5-599a-907d-e5751bd4bc1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362326 #edb/ref 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we need to find the equilibrium solutions. So, we solve $f(y)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =0 \\\\ (y-3)({y}^{2}+3y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibrium solution is $y=3.$ \n\nNow, we analyze the sign of $f(y)$ using a sign table, as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y-3$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| ${y}^{2}+3y+9$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (3,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-\\infty ,3).$\n\nWe can now draw the phase line for this equation.\n\n![](images/24/244a46b17ad69e8d7cc3ae4f3d9c3c46c1d6e6a7ea154b849946f8ece6030071.png)\n\nFinally, we classify the equilibrium solution:\n\n- $y=3$ is a source because the nearby solutions diverge away from this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$y=3$ is a source\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362330 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362330 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98774" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362330 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362330 #edb/ref 1071 "A particular autonomous differential equation has the following properties:\n\n- $y=0$ is a source\n\n- $y=-4$ is a sink\n\nWhich of the following could be the slope field of the differential equation?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362330 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2a6368a-0d2f-5f5f-a7f7-7f9694dc9333" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362330 #edb/ref 1074 "We can classify the equilibrium solutions of an autonomous ODE ${y}^{′}=f(y)$ by examining its phase line.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *sink* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *toward* the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *source* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line both point *away* from the equilibrium solution.\n\n- An equilibrium solution is a *node* if the arrows directly above and below it on the phase line point in the same direction.\n\nFirst, we draw the phase line with the given information. \n\nNote that there are $2$ equilibrium points, $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- We're given that $y=0$ is a source. We represent this on the phase line by drawing an upward-pointing arrow just above $y=0$ and a downward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n- We're given that $y=-4$ is a sink. We represent this on the phase line by drawing a downward-pointing arrow just above $y=-4$ (already drawn by the above property) and an upward-pointing arrow just below it.\n\n![](images/e5/e5ba765b9caffab904f07c5086d3206f72e36e66cb018673ce7ed44d79d19e88.png)\n\nOf the given options, the only slope field that correspond with this phase line is the following:\n\n![](images/41/41f32a097325dbe2a278da2abe4ef93b13d058ffe49431882fade3bc40bc0f48.png)\n\nNote that:\n\n- The slope field is horizontal at $y=0$ and $y=-4.$\n\n- The slopes near $y=0$ appear to diverge away from this line.\n\n- The slopes near $y=-4$ appear to converge toward this line." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
